---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import AnalysisStarter from "../../components/guides/AnalysisStarter";
import CipherDetective from "../../components/guides/CipherDetective";
import CipherClueGrid from "../../components/guides/CipherClueGrid";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout title="Which cipher is it?">
  <header class="wrap page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE 02 / INVESTIGATE</p>
    <h1>
      Which cipher
      <br />
      is it<span class="accent">?</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Learn to read the clues before guessing. Count letters and groups, compare patterns, then
      investigate a mystery step by step.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Guide contents">
      <a href="#starter">Start with counting</a>
      <a href="#explore">Explore four patterns</a>
      <a href="#cheat-sheet">Keep a clue grid</a>
      <a href="#next">Choose your next test</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="guide-content">
      <div id="starter">
        <AnalysisStarter client:load />
      </div>
      <p>
        Ready to investigate? Use plenty of text if you can. Cipher blocks of five letters usually
        make a message easier to copy; they do not normally mark five-letter words. Our analysis
        removes those display spaces.
      </p>
      <div id="explore">
        <CipherDetective client:load />
      </div>
      <h2 id="cheat-sheet">Keep this clue grid beside your puzzle</h2>
      <p>
        ✅ means “usually yes to this clue”; ❌ means “usually no”. It does not mean that a cipher
        is good or bad. Read along a row to see the combination of clues, rather than deciding from
        one tick.
      </p>
      <CipherClueGrid />
      <p>
        A Caesar cipher with shift zero is unchanged, and a one-letter Vigenère key behaves like
        Caesar. Very short messages and unusual vocabulary can blur these patterns. If a clue does
        not fit, keep another possibility open.
      </p>
      <h2 id="next">Choose one experiment you can check</h2>
      <p>
        For Caesar, try a shared shift and read the result. For substitution, test a few linked
        letter guesses everywhere. For transposition, keep the letters and try moving their
        positions. For Vigenère, investigate a repeating key length. Each experiment should give you
        evidence you can explain to a partner.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}/tools/#frequency`}>
          Test your own message ↗
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}/guides/vigenere/`}>
          Learn to break Vigenère →
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}/decks/cipher-analysis/`}>
          Present the deck →
        </a>
      </div>
      <aside class="source-notes">
        <h3>Sources and practice material</h3>
        <p>
          The examples were written for this site, and the animations are shared with its
          cipher-analysis deck. English group percentages use{" "}
          <a href="https://www.norvig.com/mayzner.html">Peter Norvig’s Google Books counts</a>,
          which count within words. Our tools also include groups across removed spaces. Method
          references:{" "}
          <a href="https://cs.uri.edu/cryptography/classical-ciphers/vigenere/cryptanalysis-article.html">
            University of Rhode Island’s cryptanalysis notes
          </a>{" "}
          and{" "}
          <a href="https://www.cs.trincoll.edu/cryptography/vigenere.html">
            Trinity College’s Vigenère tutorial
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </aside>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
